course: world
lesson: 169
title: Bets, dares and silly reasons
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Charlie bets Noah a chocolate bar that he can hold his breath for five minutes: "My cousin can do it." But his cousin doing it (maybe) says nothing about Charlie, and last time Charlie went red at 30 seconds. Breath-holding contests are dangerous, so this is a bet to walk away from.
explain: Bets and dares are full of silly reasons: a dream as proof ("I did it in my dream"), a calendar as a weather forecast (snow because it's Christmas), new trainers as superpowers, a big brother as the expert.
explain: Dares often come with a false choice: "If you don't, you're scared." There are other reasons to say no: it tastes awful, it's dangerous, it's just pointless. Saying no is choosing, not being scared.
explain: Some dares invent deals nobody agreed to: "If you don't climb on the roof, you owe me a fiver." A bet needs two people to agree, and you can't owe money because someone said a sentence at you.
explain: The coolest comebacks aim at the reason: "If it's not on, you touch it." The kid who dares you to do something risky usually won't do it himself, because he doesn't believe his own claim.
fact: Someone else being able to do something does not mean you can; grown-ups call this a false comparison.
fact: Most children can hold their breath for about 30 seconds to a minute; breath-holding contests, especially in water, are dangerous and should never be done.
fact: "Do it or you're scared" is a false choice (an either-or): there are other reasons to say no.
fact: A dream is not practice and not evidence; jumping from high up can break bones.
fact: A question built so that every answer looks wrong is a trick, or loaded, question; it's fine to say "that's a trick question".
fact: Snow on Christmas Day is rare in most of Britain now, and in London it hardly ever settles; the forecast is a far better guide than the calendar.
fact: Never touch or test an electric fence; you cannot tell by looking whether it is on.
fact: If the person daring you won't do it themselves, they don't really believe their own claim.
fact: Good running shoes can help a little if they fit, but training matters far more for speed.
fact: Scientists have trained goldfish to remember things for weeks and even months; the "three-second memory" is a myth.
fact: A bet needs both people to agree; nobody owes anything for a deal they never accepted.
fact: In science a fruit is the part of a plant that holds the seeds, so tomatoes are fruits; cooks call them vegetables because they are not sweet.
fact: Words people use: "nah, I'm good" means no thanks, "bro" and "mate" mean friend, "mid" means not very good (young slang), "a fiver" is a five-pound note, "galaxy brain" is sarcastic for a silly idea, and "your face will fold in half" is a joke about the sour face lemons give you.
exercise: Charlie says he can hold his breath for five minutes because his cousin can. What is the broken step? | His cousin doing it doesn't mean Charlie can | His cousin is older, so it's easier for him | Five minutes is easy for anyone | Holding your breath gets easier when you bet
exercise: How long can most children hold their breath? | About 30 seconds to a minute | About five minutes | About ten minutes | About three seconds
exercise: Mo is dared to eat a whole lemon: "If you don't, you're scared." What is the trap? | A false choice: there are other reasons to say no | A false comparison between Mo and his older cousin | A trick question about lemons that has no right answer | A bet that Mo had already agreed to before the dare
exercise: Kai says he can jump from the top of the climbing frame because he did it in a dream. What is wrong? | A dream is not practice or evidence | Dreams only work if you have them twice | He should have dreamed it from higher up | Climbing frames are made for jumping
exercise: Ellie asks, "Are you going to say no?" and dares you to answer "yes". What kind of question is it? | A trick question with no good answer | A fair question with one right answer | A maths question about yes and no | A question you must answer honestly
exercise: Harry bets it will snow on Christmas Day in London "because it's Christmas". What is a better guide? | The weather forecast | Christmas cards | Last year's Christmas film | How much he wants snow
exercise: Lily dares Jade to touch the electric fence: "It's not even on, I bet." What is true? | Never test an electric fence; you can't tell if it's on | It's safe if someone bets it's off | Electric fences are only ever switched on at night, so daytime is fine | Touching it quickly is always fine
exercise: Sam says he'll win the race because he has new trainers. What matters most for speed? | Training | The colour of the trainers | How new the shoes are | The brand on the side
exercise: Ava bets that goldfish have a three-second memory "because my brother said". What did scientists find? | Goldfish can remember things for weeks or months | Goldfish forget everything in three seconds | Goldfish have no memory at all | Goldfish remember only food, and forget everything else straight away
exercise: Finn says, "Climb on the roof, or you owe me a fiver." Why don't you owe him anything? | A bet needs both people to agree | Fivers are not really proper money | He said it on a school day, so it doesn't count | You only ever owe money to grown-ups
exercise: Rosie says a tomato is a vegetable because it goes in salad. Who is right? | Both, using different meanings of the word | Only Rosie, because cooks decide | Only her friend, because salads are always made of fruit | Neither, a tomato is a nut
exercise: Why is a tomato a fruit in science? | It is the part of the plant that holds the seeds | It is sweet like other fruits | It grows on a tree | It is red when it's ripe
exercise: What do grown-ups call "someone else can, so I can"? | A false comparison | A fair test | A trick question | A safe bet
exercise: What is a good sign that a dare is a bad idea? | The person daring you won't do it themselves | The dare is said loudly | Lots of kids are watching | The prize is chocolate
exercise: What is Mo really doing if he says no to the lemon? | Choosing, not being scared | Losing the bet and owing money | Admitting he is a coward | Breaking a playground rule
test: Noah: "Your cousin's a dolphin now, is he? You went red at 30 seconds last time." What is the right thing to do with this bet? | Drop it: breath-holding contests are dangerous | Practise every day until you can manage the full five minutes | Try it in the swimming pool instead | Bet a bigger chocolate bar
test: Mo: "I'm not scared, I just don't want my face to fold in half. Next." What is he showing? | Saying no to a dare can be a choice with a reason | He is far too scared to admit it, so he makes up an excuse | Lemons are dangerous to touch | He will do the dare tomorrow
test: His mate: "In my dream I flew to Spain. Doesn't mean I'm skipping the plane, does it." Why is it a good comeback? | It shows dreams don't prove what you can do | It shows that his mate really can fly in real life | It means Spain is actually very close to London | It proves dreams come true when you go on holiday
test: Friend: "My brain just crashed. Did I lose? Was there even a game? This is so mid." What is the best response to Ellie's question? | Laugh and say, "That's a trick question." | Keep trying and trying until you find an answer that works | Say yes and then say no | Agree you lost the bet
test: Jake: "Mate, it hardly ever snows in London on Christmas Day. You're betting on a Christmas card." What does he mean? | Christmas pictures show snow, but real weather doesn't follow them | Christmas cards can predict the weather, because the artists check the forecast | It snows every Christmas outside London | Snow only falls on Christmas Eve
test: Jade: "If it's not on, you touch it." Lily: "...Nah, I'm good." What does Lily's answer show? | She doesn't believe her own claim | She is completely sure the fence is off | She is a lot braver than Jade will ever be | She wants to touch it a bit later on
test: Racing friend, after winning: "Lovely shoes though, Sam. Really fast-looking." What is being teased? | The idea that new shoes make you fast | Sam's taste in shoes | Sam for being slow on purpose | The shop that sold the shoes
test: Mia: "Your brother's not Google, Ava." What would be a better way to settle the goldfish bet? | Look it up with a good source, like the teacher did | Ask her brother again more loudly | Watch a cartoon about fish | Guess an answer and see who can shout it the loudest and longest
test: Oscar: "You can't invent a debt. That's not a bet, that's a scam." What is true? | Nobody owes for a deal they never agreed to | You must pay if someone dares you | Fivers are only for dares | Roof dares are fine, as long as you get paid a fiver for doing them
test: Teacher: "Botanically, it's a fruit. In the kitchen people treat it as a vegetable." Then is ketchup a smoothie? | No, ketchup is still a sauce, whatever the tomato is | Yes, because tomatoes are fruit, so anything made from them is a smoothie | Yes, but only when it's cold | No, because tomatoes are vegetables
test: What would happen if everybody followed the rule "do every dare or you're scared"? | Kids would end up doing dangerous things for no reason | Everyone would quickly become braver, and safer too, in the end | Nobody would ever get hurt | Dares would stop being fun
exam: Identify the principal error in the claim that a child is able to hold his breath for five minutes because his cousin is able to do so. | An ability of one person is wrongly transferred to another | The cousin is insufficiently related to the child | Five minutes is an insufficient duration | Chocolate is an unsuitable stake
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the memory of goldfish. | Goldfish have been trained to remember for weeks and months | Goldfish retain memories for three seconds only | Goldfish possess no memory whatsoever | Goldfish remember solely the colour of their food, and nothing else at all
exam: Determine the correct classification of the tomato in the science of plants. | A fruit, being the seed-bearing part of the plant | A vegetable, being served in salads and cooked with other vegetables | A root, being grown in soil | A leaf, being green when unripe
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the assertion "If you do not do it, you are scared"? | A false choice which ignores other reasons for refusal | A fair test of courage | A binding wager agreed by both parties | A statement supported by evidence
